WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.710
So, another important concept when it

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:04.070
comes to working with these agents is

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.910
the context window. And the context

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.630
window, you can basically think of it

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.870
back to our analogy where if these AI

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.030
agents are your digital employees and

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.030
you give them their own desks, the

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.590
context window is basically that desk

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.070
that continues to fill up the more that

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.150
you talk to your agent. But the thing is

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.950
that window actually has a limit and

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.990
once that limit of tokens is reached,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.190
then your chat with that agent for that

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.390
particular session is now going to be

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.630
full. And just to crystallize this a bit

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.510
further, in Claude code, if I go to the

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.830
model options here and I switch a model,

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.910
you can see here that Opus has a 1

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.070
million context window. So, what that

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.310
basically means is if I continue

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.550
chatting with this agent and we fill up

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.150
this whole context, this whole table

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.110
with tokens, which right now you can see

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.790
I sent an input prompt here, which is

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.750
roughly maybe five tokens and then it

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.190
sent back a reply to me with maybe

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.670
roughly 200 tokens given the length of

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.110
this piece that it sent. If we just

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.750
continue chatting and it actually

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.390
reaches 1 million tokens in length, then

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.310
this whole session will now be filled

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.670
up, right? And so you can see here

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.350
Claude code explaining, the reason why

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.110
it matters is that once it is full, then

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.990
something has to give. For Claude code,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.870
what happens is that all the older

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.590
content actually gets summarized and it

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.350
does that through this process called

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.910
compaction, which you can actually

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.030
invoke yourself if in case you want to

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.110
compact your conversation. If you just

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.710
type in {slash} compact, what will

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.870
happen there is your whole session will

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.670
just be summarized and that is done just

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.230
by the AI actually looking at the whole

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.150
corpus of text and giving itself a

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.190
prompt to summarize it. So, you can see

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.670
here because there's not a lot of

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.110
messages to compact, it actually refused

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.510
to do that. But let's say this other

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.390
session where it actually created a

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.030
landing page for us, if I type in

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.590
{slash} compact, then you'll be able to

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.190
squeeze that whole conversation so that

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.310
more stuff will fit into [snorts] the

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.350
context window of this model. And the

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.470
reason why it's important to compact a

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.630
conversation is because of the concept

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.910
of context rot. And simply put, what

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.230
that means is that the longer a session

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.469
runs, it actually gets perceivably

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.230
dumber. And the reason why is because

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.990
the window, the context window, starts

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.630
to fill with a lot of tokens, starts to

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.870
fill with maybe irrelevant information.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.030
Let's say if you're conversing with it a

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.350
lot, it already has dead ends, stale

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.150
details, old errors, and so on. So,

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.630
slowly but surely, AI starts to lose the

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.630
plot, and it's actually having a harder

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.830
time to look for the exact thing that

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.830
you want during that session versus if

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.750
you just start a fresh one. And this is

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.630
sort of similar if you're trying to find

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.590
like a passage in a book, right? So, a 1

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.510
million token context is almost similar

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.070
to the length of seven Harry Potter

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.230
books put side by side together. And you

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.350
can imagine if the conversation is

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.910
already that long, then if you ask about

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.870
a particular topic, then even though the

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.110
model is smart, it may find that topic

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.270
let's say in chapter two when the one

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.270
that you were looking for is the topic

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.110
in chapter 10. And it's just because

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.430
that whole conversation has gone on for

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.590
too long already. And so, in practice,

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.350
if in that session you're finding that

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.350
there is a bit of perceivable context

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.949
rot already, then you can do a {slash}

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:12.870
compact in order to summarize the

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.830
conversation. But in reality, most of

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.310
the time because these models are

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.310
already quite good and are actually

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.030
getting smarter on the day, you can

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.070
actually just let the session run up

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.990
until you see some context rot

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.110
happening. And in a way, by the end of

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.790
the context window, let's say this whole

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.990
chat fills up with 1 million tokens, it

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.550
will actually compact it on its own. The

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.390
other thing that is quite useful as a

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.110
command is if you type in {slash}

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.630
context, what that will do is show you

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.270
how much of your context window has

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.949
already filled up. So, you can see here

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:43.870
that that much of the window has already

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.190
been used, but do note that the messages

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.830
that you see in your Claude code session

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.790
are only included here in the purple

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.830
area. And you can see we only consume

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.510
7,000 tokens so far. Because the reality

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.830
of each Claude code session is that even

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.430
before you talk to it, there is already

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.830
a system prompt that Anthropic has

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.830
pre-built in or pre-injected or

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.230
pre-coded in the Claude code harness,

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.390
which we cannot change. It also has

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.270
these system tools, which again we

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.949
cannot change. And apart from that, it

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:15.790
has your memory files as well as skills,

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.030
which we will tackle on a later course.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.390
Key point being that before you even

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.390
talk to your agent, you actually already

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.030
have something like 20, 25,000 tokens

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.790
already consumed or even bigger,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.870
depending on the size of your workspace.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.070
But the good news is this token context

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.270
window is actually quite large and is

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:33.909
probably going to get larger as time

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:35.550
goes on. So at least what's present in

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.750
the industry now is that this context

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.909
window is not the biggest blocker for

00:04:39.919 --> 00:04:42.480
most.
